        "id": "p14-q66-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Show that the coefficient of $x^n$ in $(1+x)^{2n}$ is double the coefficient of $x^n$ in $(1+x)^{2n-1}$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\binom{2n}{n}=2\\binom{2n-1}{n}$, by expanding the coefficients into factorials.",
          "worked": "The two coefficients are $\\binom{2n}{n}$ and $\\binom{2n-1}{n}$ respectively.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\binom{2n}{n}&=\\frac{(2n)!}{n!\\,n!}\\\\\\binom{2n}{n}&=\\frac{2n(2n-1)!}{n!\\,n(n-1)!}\\\\\\binom{2n}{n}&=2\\frac{(2n-1)!}{n!\\,(n-1)!}\\\\\\binom{2n}{n}&=2\\binom{2n-1}{n}.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 46
      },
      {
        "id": "p14-q66-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
